In C, you can terminate a program a few common ways:

```c
return 0;
```

From `main()`, this exits successfully:

```c
int main(void) {
    // game loop / logic
    return 0;
}
```

For an error exit, return nonzero:

```c
return 1;
```

Or use `exit()` from `<stdlib.h>`—useful when you need to quit from inside another function:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

void fatal_error(void) {
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

Key calls:

- `exit(EXIT_SUCCESS)` — clean successful shutdown; flushes stdio buffers and runs registered cleanup handlers.
- `exit(EXIT_FAILURE)` — clean failure shutdown.
- `abort()` — immediately ends abnormally, typically producing a crash/core dump; use for unrecoverable bugs.
- `_Exit(status)` — terminates immediately without flushing buffers or running cleanup handlers.

Most of the time, just `return 0;` at the end of `main()` is the clean GG.
